  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124422,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 24,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:30:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124423,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 24,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Lobules of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading extend from the base of the epidermis, and occasionally the adnexal epithelium, into the papillary dermis {23627639; 30741348}. Retraction of lobules from surrounding fibromyxoid stroma and a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate may be seen {11176057}. Lesions may appear multifocal but are connected in three dimensions {3631446}. Admixed regions of nodular, micronodular, and infiltrative BCC may occur.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>BerEP4/EpCAM is positive in BCC but negative in actinic keratosis {10816226}. Follicular induction resembling superficial BCC may be seen over dermatofibromas, scars, and other reactive or neoplastic conditions {25222196}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:30:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
